\section{Client creation}

The Zulip client reads credentials from a \texttt{zuliprc} file,
typically located at \texttt{\~{}/.zuliprc}.
We default to that path but allow callers to override it---the CLI
uses this to support its \texttt{-{}-zuliprc} flag.

We chose not to cache the client as a module-level singleton because
the CLI only creates one client per invocation anyway, and caching
would complicate testing (stale state between test runs).

<<imports>>=
import os
import zulip
@

<<constants>>=
DEFAULT_ZULIPRC = os.path.expanduser("~/.zuliprc")
@

An API key is the one credential worth keeping out of the filesystem
entirely, so [[get_client]] accepts an [[api_key]] and an [[email]]
alongside the file path.
This works because [[zulip.Client]] resolves each field
independently---constructor argument first, then the environment, then
the configuration file---rather than picking a single source for the
whole set.
A caller may therefore supply just the key and leave the account
address and the server URL in a [[zuliprc]] that holds no secret at
all.

Passing [[None]] is how the library spells \enquote{not specified},
which is why both overrides default to [[None]] and are forwarded
unconditionally: no branch is needed to tell \enquote{no override}
apart from \enquote{override with nothing}.
They are keyword-only because a third positional string at a call site
would be indistinguishable from a path.

<<functions>>=
def get_client(zuliprc=None, *, api_key=None, email=None):
    """Create and return a configured Zulip API client.

    Args:
        zuliprc: Path to a zuliprc configuration file.
            Defaults to `~/.zuliprc`.
        api_key: API key overriding the one in the file.
        email: Account address overriding the one in the file.

    Returns:
        A `zulip.Client` instance ready for API calls.
    """
    if zuliprc is None:
        zuliprc = DEFAULT_ZULIPRC
    return zulip.Client(
        config_file=zuliprc,
        api_key=api_key,
        email=email,
    )
@

Because [[DEFAULT_ZULIPRC]] is substituted before the call, the library
never sees [[config_file=None]] and so never consults its own
[[ZULIP_CONFIG]] variable.
That is deliberate---this package owns the question of which file to
read, and one answer is easier to reason about than two---but it is
worth stating, because the library documents [[ZULIP_CONFIG]] and a
reader would otherwise expect it to take effect here.
